  "query_preview": "..\n\nLast vestige of the quiet and dry conditions remain in place over \nsoutheast MI this afternoon and surface high slides to the east.\nAlthough, there may be a shower near the Lake Huron lake breeze late\nthis afternoon and early evening. Region's weather will be \nincreasingly influenced by a few upper waves from IL extending \nsouthwest, all in advance of the main upper low over southern \nMN/northern IA.\n\nClouds increase this evening ahead of these features first, and then \nenough moisture advection in the mid layers with one of those waves \nin the southwest flow will bring chances of showers and slight \nchance of thunderstorms around and after midnight for the northwest \nhalf of the forecast area...mainly northwest of a OZW to Sandusky \nline. \n\nThe forecast area will continue to be on the edge of the best \nforcing of the upper trof on Saturday as a cold front slides across \nnorthern MI. A surface low will move into IN in response to the \nupper trof with a warm frontal feature extending to the east into \nOH. Closer to these surface and low level forcing features will be \nthe best chances of showers and a few thunderstorms to develop and \nwill have the highest POPs in the Tri Cities and down near the OH \nborder. Regardless, it will be cloudy and humid with temps below late\nJuly averages.\n\nThe real impact part of this system occurs Saturday night and Sunday \nmorning as the upper trof moves over MI, IN and OH. PWAT increases \nto 1.75+ inches. Mid layer instability increases with around 500 \nJ/kg available, enough for convection and a chance of thunderstorms.\nAll of these environmental conditions will be greater over northern \nIN, far southeast Lower MI (ARB to PHN on south) and NW OH. The \nquestion for the heavy rain potential will be where exactly the \ndeformation axis setup a potential SHARS type of rainfall event. \nModel camps are split across the MI state line, but the potential is \nhigh enough to start messaging the heavy rain threat through DSS \nchannels. Will hold off on any Flood watches since the peak of the\nevent is still around 36 hours away to allow for some consensus on \nthat deformation axis to setup.\n\nBy Sunday afternoon, the system is slowly moving east and expect \nsome improving conditions. Those will range from partial clearing in \nMidland County to the ending of the showers in Detroit and Monroe.\n\nThe extended forecast is looking a little more unsettled for next \nweek. That upper trof now looks to cutoff over the Upper OH river \nvalley on Sunday night and Monday. While we will be far enough away \non Monday to be dry with partly sunny conditions, the cutoff low \nwill slowly migrate north on Tuesday and Wednesday bringing some \nchances for mainly diurnal showers. Later Wednesday into Thursday, \nthe next wave and cold front settle through Lower MI and stalls near \nthe southern border with additional waves in the zonal upper flow. \nThis will bring some periodic chance for showers and storms late next\nweek. All of this will have temps at or below early August averages.",
